{
  "durationSeconds": 208,
  "bytes": 14228710,
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "s01",
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 27.391,
      "title": "Where does the multiplication happen?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s02",
      "start": 27.391,
      "end": 60.235,
      "title": "Load the input."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03",
      "start": 60.235,
      "end": 91.308,
      "title": "Multiply the register value."
    },
    {
      "id": "s04",
      "start": 91.308,
      "end": 112.292,
      "title": "Write the result back."
    },
    {
      "id": "s05",
      "start": 112.292,
      "end": 137.144,
      "title": "Another thread. Separate values."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06",
      "start": 137.144,
      "end": 167.656,
      "title": "The hardware is shared."
    },
    {
      "id": "s07",
      "start": 167.656,
      "end": 183.633,
      "title": "Pause and explain."
    },
    {
      "id": "s08",
      "start": 183.633,
      "end": 208.165,
      "title": "Registers first. Memory after the store."
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "id": "s01-c1",
      "start": 0.6,
      "end": 4.12,
      "text": "In Chapter One, the GPU doubles five input values."
    },
    {
      "id": "s01-c2",
      "start": 4.34,
      "end": 8.18,
      "text": "Let\u2019s follow one of them: one point zero becomes two point zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "s01-c3",
      "start": 8.4,
      "end": 15.504,
      "text": "The GPU program supplies instructions: individual operations such as a load, a multiply, or a store."
    },
    {
      "id": "s01-c4",
      "start": 15.724,
      "end": 21.42,
      "text": "A streaming multiprocessor, or S M, provides the hardware that performs those instructions."
    },
    {
      "id": "s01-c5",
      "start": 21.64,
      "end": 25.971,
      "text": "The input and output arrays are in GPU memory, outside the S M."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c1",
      "start": 27.991,
      "end": 31.809,
      "text": "Thread Zero\u2019s load requests the value at input index zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c2",
      "start": 32.029,
      "end": 35.101,
      "text": "Load and store units handle memory instructions."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c3",
      "start": 35.321,
      "end": 42.148,
      "text": "A load brings a value from memory into a register: a small, fast storage location inside the S M."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c4",
      "start": 42.368,
      "end": 46.592,
      "text": "Registers hold the working values that arithmetic instructions use."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c5",
      "start": 46.812,
      "end": 52.999,
      "text": "The request can take time; this diagram jumps ahead to when one point zero arrives in a register."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c6",
      "start": 53.219,
      "end": 56.099,
      "text": "The input array still holds its original value."
    },
    {
      "id": "s02-c7",
      "start": 56.319,
      "end": 58.815,
      "text": "Nothing has been written to the output array yet."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c1",
      "start": 60.835,
      "end": 65.379,
      "text": "The multiply instruction uses the loaded value and the multiplier two."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c2",
      "start": 65.599,
      "end": 70.356,
      "text": "Floating point arithmetic units perform calculations on floating point values."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c3",
      "start": 70.576,
      "end": 74.715,
      "text": "Here, they produce two point zero and return the result to a register."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c4",
      "start": 74.935,
      "end": 79.393,
      "text": "Our values use thirty two bit floating point, often called F P thirty two."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c5",
      "start": 79.613,
      "end": 84.84,
      "text": "These arithmetic units are shared by threads, rather than permanently owned by Thread Zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c6",
      "start": 85.06,
      "end": 88.452,
      "text": "The multiply has not written the result to the output array."
    },
    {
      "id": "s03-c7",
      "start": 88.672,
      "end": 89.888,
      "text": "That needs a store."
    },
    {
      "id": "s04-c1",
      "start": 91.908,
      "end": 93.892,
      "text": "The store is a separate instruction."
    },
    {
      "id": "s04-c2",
      "start": 94.112,
      "end": 102.261,
      "text": "Load and store units write two point zero from the thread\u2019s result register into output index zero in GPU memory."
    },
    {
      "id": "s04-c3",
      "start": 102.481,
      "end": 104.7,
      "text": "The result is now in the output array."
    },
    {
      "id": "s04-c4",
      "start": 104.92,
      "end": 110.872,
      "text": "We have followed the whole value path: memory to register, arithmetic, then register to memory."
    },
    {
      "id": "s05-c1",
      "start": 112.892,
      "end": 116.284,
      "text": "Now inspect Thread One, whose input is two point zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "s05-c2",
      "start": 116.504,
      "end": 119.085,
      "text": "Its load supplies its own working register."
    },
    {
      "id": "s05-c3",
      "start": 119.305,
      "end": 121.609,
      "text": "Thread Zero\u2019s values remain separate."
    },
    {
      "id": "s05-c4",
      "start": 121.829,
      "end": 126.971,
      "text": "We are changing which values we inspect, not choosing the GPU\u2019s execution order."
    },
    {
      "id": "s05-c5",
      "start": 127.191,
      "end": 135.724,
      "text": "A GPU can execute instructions for groups of threads together; this picture isolates two threads to make their values easy to follow."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06-c1",
      "start": 137.744,
      "end": 143.76,
      "text": "Thread One\u2019s multiply uses the same depicted floating point hardware, producing four point zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06-c2",
      "start": 143.98,
      "end": 146.881,
      "text": "Its store writes that result to output index one."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06-c3",
      "start": 147.101,
      "end": 151.112,
      "text": "Each thread has its own working values, while execution units are shared."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06-c4",
      "start": 151.332,
      "end": 156.495,
      "text": "The input and result columns identify values, not fixed physical registers."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06-c5",
      "start": 156.715,
      "end": 160.512,
      "text": "A register can be reused after a value is no longer needed."
    },
    {
      "id": "s06-c6",
      "start": 160.732,
      "end": 166.236,
      "text": "The boxes show hardware roles, not unit counts or exactly when instructions execute."
    },
    {
      "id": "s07-c1",
      "start": 168.256,
      "end": 169.557,
      "text": "Try one more case."
    },
    {
      "id": "s07-c2",
      "start": 169.777,
      "end": 173.489,
      "text": "A third thread loads three point zero and multiplies it by two."
    },
    {
      "id": "s07-c3",
      "start": 173.709,
      "end": 175.587,
      "text": "Its store instruction has not run."
    },
    {
      "id": "s07-c4",
      "start": 175.807,
      "end": 180.308,
      "text": "Where is the result at this point: in a working register, or in the output array?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s07-c5",
      "start": 180.528,
      "end": 182.213,
      "text": "Pause here and explain why."
    },
    {
      "id": "s08-c1",
      "start": 184.233,
      "end": 187.647,
      "text": "The result, six point zero, is in a working register."
    },
    {
      "id": "s08-c2",
      "start": 187.867,
      "end": 190.384,
      "text": "The store is what writes it to the output array."
    },
    {
      "id": "s08-c3",
      "start": 190.604,
      "end": 197.196,
      "text": "That is the distinction to take back to Chapter One: threads describe executions with separate working values."
    },
    {
      "id": "s08-c4",
      "start": 197.416,
      "end": 201.533,
      "text": "The S M provides shared hardware for loads, arithmetic and stores."
    },
    {
      "id": "s08-c5",
      "start": 201.753,
      "end": 206.745,
      "text": "This is a conceptual explanation, not a trace of the kernel PyTorch selected."
    }
  ],
